Refine the existing Dashboard into the production UI for \*\*CoreOps\*\*, a professional Linux server management application.

Replace all visible “Server Control Center” branding with \*\*CoreOps\*\*, including the application logo text, sidebar branding, header and prototype labels.

Do not create a new design from scratch. Preserve the existing sidebar structure, typography, navigation and component identity while introducing a functional \*\*Bento layout\*\* and \*\*restrained dark neumorphism\*\* inspired by the two attached button images.

Focus exclusively on the Dashboard. Do not redesign other application screens yet.

## 1. Dashboard goal

The Dashboard must answer this question within approximately 5 seconds:

“Is my server healthy, and if not, what needs my attention?”

Prioritize operational information, clear states and useful actions. Avoid generic analytics layouts and decorative charts.

## 2. Visual direction from the reference images

Translate these qualities into the existing interface:

- Matte charcoal background, approximately #212121.
- Graphite surfaces with subtle tonal separation.
- Soft highlights from the upper left and shadows toward the lower right.
- Restrained bevels and inset edges that create a tactile appearance.
- Muted blue, approximately #4568CC, for selected and active controls.
- Soft silver-gray for neutral icons and inactive controls.

The first image demonstrates an active control: blue icon with a subtle blue edge treatment.

The second image demonstrates an inactive control: gray icon and a darker inset ring.

An inactive control remains available for interaction. Design a separate disabled state.

Apply this material language selectively:

- Cards: shallow, subtle elevation.
- Inputs and segmented controls: lightly recessed surfaces.
- Primary controls: more pronounced tactile depth.
- Pressed controls: a subtle inset appearance.
- Charts and dense information areas: mostly flat surfaces for clarity.

Use the reference buttons as material and interaction references. Do not repeat their circular shape throughout the interface or add a power control solely because it appears in the images.

Avoid heavy shadows around every element, glossy materials, neon, glassmorphism and exaggerated 3D effects. Ensure boundaries and interactive states remain identifiable without relying exclusively on shadows.

## 3. Functional Bento layout

Refine the existing grid into a compact Bento composition with aligned modular cards.

Determine card sizes by content and operational importance. Use consistent padding, spacing and moderate corner radii.

Organize the Dashboard into:

- Header and health status.
- Four compact resource metric cards.
- CPU and Memory chart cards.
- System information and Containers overview.
- Recent activity and Quick Actions.

Avoid oversized tiles, unnecessary nested containers and empty space added purely to complete a symmetrical grid.

